Thanks very much for the author's reply. The experimental results and analysis in the reply did not alleviate my concerns, so I choose to keep my original score. My concern is still in the experimental results:
1. The implementation of the official AWP is to perturb all model parameters, not as the author described, increasing K will increase the number of perturbed model parameters;
2. The experimental parameters of the official AWP are used for adversarial training. In my opinion, it is necessary to at least adjust the value of $\gamma$ in AWP for the new task. We can see that even if the hyperparameters are directly transferred to the new task, AWP (K=4) can achieve similar results to Oracle;
3. What I said in the initial comment was "perturbing a very small number of model weights to increase the classification loss of deleted data", the author seems to have overlooked this point;
4. I strongly recommend that the author try to follow what I said, select some unimportant parameters, and then use RWP (a multi-step version of AWP) to perturb the selected model parameters until the misclassification goal is achieved. At the same time, adjust the $\gamma$ value when necessary.
